{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":24593,"output":19942,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":18323,"totalTokens":44535,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Good understanding. Note: the static checker treats `module` as known global. Also note it flags duplicate top-level let/const/class across classic scripts.\n\nImportant detail: `elevator_logic.js` uses IIFE with `(function(root){...})(...)`. Inside, `class ElevatorLogic` is declared inside a function scope, so not root — fine. But `ElevatorLogic` must be accessible in elevator.js as global via window.ElevatorLogic; static checker resolves refs through projectGlobals which includes names assigned to window.X = X (only if pattern `window.<ident> =`). So I should add `window.ElevatorLogic = ElevatorLogic;` inside the IIFE — that's fine, and also referenced in elevator.js as `ElevatorLogic`. Since `window.ElevatorLogic = ElevatorLogic` adds it to projectGlobals, references elsewhere resolve. Good.\n\nAlso careful: static checker flags refs like `THREE` (known global). Good.\n\nLet me plan the implementation. This is a big build. I'll write files myself carefully — this is a single-writer scenario; delegation isn't authorized by the user prompt (the prompt says use agentic coding tools available... but the harness rules say delegate only when operator asked). The user gave the full spec to me. I'll implement directly, maybe use subagents for tests? Better to do it myself carefully — quality matters most.\n\nLet me design.\n\nArchitecture plan:\n\nConstants (shared): define in world.js as `const WORLD = {...}` global. Also need FLOOR_HEIGHT etc. sim.js uses WORLD.\n\nperson.js:\n- palettes\n- createPerson({bodyColor, skinColor, legColor}) -> THREE.Group with legs groups pivoting at hip, arms at shoulder, head + nose hemisphere.\n  Structure: origin at feet (y=0). Legs length ~0.8; hip at y=0.8; torso from 0.8 to 1.4; head at 1.5-1.75. Arms pivot at shoulder y=1.35.\n  Implementation: legGroup = new THREE.Group(); legGroup.position.set(±0.12, hipY, 0); cylinder mesh with position.y = -legLen/2 (so it hangs below pivot). rotation.x rotates about hip. Good.\n- animatePersonWalking(person, dt).\n\nworld.js:\n- WORLD constants.\n- createWorld(scene) builds buildingGroup; nodes per floor; sitTargets; callPanel; shaftIndicator; bfsPath.\n- Navigation graph: nodes as {name, pos (Vector3), links:[names]}. Per-floor node names must be unique across floors? bfsPath(nodes, fromName, toName) takes a floor's nodes array. sim.js will use per-floor nodes. For \"from agent's current world position\" — need a nearest-node lookup: add helper `nearestNode(nodes, pos)` and link the graph. I'll implement bfsPath with an optional start node name; plus helper function `pathFromPoint(floorNodes, startPoint, toName)`: find nearest node on that floor, then BFS.\n\nCareful about duplicate top-level const across scripts: WORLD in world.js only. In sim.js avoid re-declaring names like `world`, `elevator` — those are declared at root in sim.js; fine as long as not duplicated elsewhere. elevator.js declares class Elevator; world.js declares function createWorld, bfsPath... careful: if I declare `function bfsPath` at top-level in world.js and also use it in sim.js, that's fine (projectGlobals includes function names).\n\nPotential duplicate issue: e.g., `const FLOOR_HEIGHT` in both world.js and elevator.js → error. So use WORLD.FLOOR_HEIGHT everywhere.\n\nAlso careful with common helper names like `makeTexture`, `createPanel` — declare unique names per file to avoid duplicates across files. I'll prefix names: e.g. `worldMakeTextTexture`. Actually duplicate top-level function declarations are not flagged (only let/const/class). But to be safe, use unique names.\n\nLet me now think about the elevator logic design carefully since tests must pass.\n\nElevatorLogic class:\n```js\nclass ElevatorLogic {\n  constructor(opts={}){\n    this.floorCount = opts.floorCount ?? 6;\n    this.maxCapacity = opts.maxCapacity ?? 4;\n    this.floorHeight = opts.floorHeight ?? 3.4;\n    this.minDoorOpenS = ... ; maxDoorOpenS...\n    this.speed = ... (floors per second?) \n    states: 'IDLE','MOVING','DOOR_OPENING','DOOR_OPEN','DOOR_CLOSING'\n    this.state='IDLE'; this.direction=0; this.currentFloor=0; this.targetFloor=0;\n    this.upCalls=new Set(); downCalls; destinations (Set of floors? or Map floor->count?). Use Set of floors. passengers = Set; pendingBoarders=Set; pendingDisembark=Set; spotOccupancy array length 4 (null or person).\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDoor timers: doorTimer, state transitions with durations: DOOR_OPENING duration ~1.0s, DOOR_CLOSING ~1.0s, MIN_DOOR_OPEN_S = 2.5? Actually min door open should be enough for boarding; but since pending sets hold doors open, min can be small-ish. Let's set MIN_DOOR_OPEN_S = 3, MAX_DOOR_OPEN_S = 12.\n\nTravel: moving at speed floors/sec — say 1.5 floors per second (i.e., ~0.9s per floor). Use position in floor units: `this.carFloorPos` continuous; when |pos - target| < eps → arrival → DOOR_OPENING.\n\nHmm, but tests need deterministic behavior with tick(dt). Fine.\n\npickNextTarget rules:\n- If destinations.size > 0 and passengers.size>0 → nearest destination in current direction ahead, else any destination.\n- Else consider calls in current direction; if none, reverse; etc.\n- Guard `servedThisDoorCycle` / lastServedFloor to prevent reopening same floor while destinations exist.\n\nKey rule: \"No full-car lobby starvation\": at door-close transition, if the car is full or has passenger destinations, don't pick the current floor again even if a call exists there. Implement: when choosing target, exclude `currentFloor` unless nothing else exists AND no passengers/destinations... Actually careful: a hall call at the current floor with empty car should be served (people boarding). But after doors close and boarders boarded, we shouldn't reopen same floor. The rule \"same floor cannot reopen indefinitely while destinations exist\" — implement `servedThisDoorCycle` flag reset when leaving a floor.\n\nLet me define pickTarget():\n```\npickTarget() {\n  const cur = this.currentFloor;\n  // passenger destinations outrank hall calls\n  if (this.destinations.size) {\n     // nearest destination in current direction, else nearest overall\n     ...\n     return best;\n  }\n  // hall calls\n  const dir = this.direction;\n  if (dir !== 0) {\n    scan floors from cur+dir..bounds for matching call; if found return.\n    then reverse: scan opposite direction for any call.\n  }\n  scan both directions nearest call, infer direction.\n  return null -> IDLE park.\n}\n```\nAnd guard: never return `cur` at the door-close transition (that's a reopen). Actually at door close, if only remaining work is a call at current floor and car has passengers/destinations → skip it (keep queued). If car empty and no destinations but a call at current floor exists... that means someone new pressed while doors were open — reopening would be reasonable but could loop. The spec says guard so same floor cannot reopen indefinitely while destinations exist. I'll implement: at door-close, if target candidate == currentFloor → skip (keep queued for next trip) unless nothing else anywhere; then it'd stay IDLE... hmm that could deadlock with empty car and only a call at current floor. Let's handle: allow reopening at same floor at most once per cycle via `reopenedAtSameFloor` flag, reset when the car moves to another floor. That satisfies tests (test 2 asserts no immediate reopen at same floor while passengers+destinations exist; test 3 asserts reaches a destination).\n\nTest 1: board exactly four people (capacity full), press destinations upper floors, leftover lobby callers re-press UP at floor 0 → after doors close next target must be above 0. With destinations present, pickTarget returns nearest destination > 0. Good. Also car is full so `currentCapacityFree()==0`.\n\nTest 4: moving up with work above; a DOWN call at current or lower floor should not reverse until upward work served. Implement scan in current direction first.\n\nTest 5: doors remain open while pending sets non-empty after min open, but close after MAX_DOOR_OPEN_S. So door-open logic: `if (doorOpenElapsed >= MIN && (pending empty)) → closing; else if doorOpenElapsed >= MAX → closing regardless`.\n\nTest 6: destination preserved across handshake — test simulates pressDestination(5), then checks destinations contains 5 and targetFloor eventually 5, not floor+dir. Our logic stores destinations as floors. Fine.\n\nTest 7: reset clears everything including spotOccupancy and door timers.\n\nAlso `isAcceptingAt(floor, direction)`: state DOOR_OPEN && (no more stops pending in current direction || caller's direction matches car). And capacity free presumably checked separately by reserveBoardingSpot.\n\nNow the visual adapter elevator.js: mirrors logic; converts logical spots to Vector3. Logic exposes spot positions? `reserveBoardingSpot` returns `{index, x, y, z}` — plain object with car-local coords. I'll define in logic a fixed layout of 4 spots (x = ±0.6, z = -0.5/0.5?). Car interior: width 3 (SHAFT_WIDTH), depth 3. Doors at +Z face. Spots: back row and middle row. Let's define spots local coords relative to car center? The car group origin — I'll place the car group at shaft center at current floor y = pos*floorHeight, with interior from x in [-1.5,1.5], z in [-1.5,1.5]. Passengers stand inside; person origin is feet, so spot y = 0 (car floor at y=0 local). Let's define spots: (-0.7,-0.6), (0.7,-0.6), (-0.7,0.4), (0.7,0.4) as {x,y,z} with y=0. Return `{index, x, y, z}`.\n\nNow sim.js — the biggest file. Agents, plans, actions, clock, lighting, HUD.\n\nKey design decisions:\n- Agent object: {id, name, role, group, floor, state, plan[], currentAction, ...}.\n- Actions as objects {type, args...}; dispatch loop handles them.\n\nWALK_TO_WP